      'knowledge-systems-architect':[
        {name:'Information architecture and concept modeling',status:'Synthesized',definition:'Model the kinds of things a system contains, the boundaries between them, and the relationships that help people find and use them.',triggers:'A new concept resembles several existing ones · Navigation feels harder to explain · Content or capability is difficult to place',workflows:'Frame the system’s concept model · Place a new concept in the architecture · Maintain the shared taxonomy and boundaries',actions:'Names the concept’s responsibility, compares it with neighboring objects, maps relationships and ownership, and tests whether the model supports retrieval and contribution.',evidence:'Synthesized from Information Architecture Institute and GOV.UK service-structure guidance · Validate through card sorts, tree tests, retrieval behavior, and contributor interviews'},
        {name:'Concept classification and boundary design',status:'Synthesized',definition:'Decide whether an addition is a new object, a relationship, a variation, or a future possibility, then define what it should and should not contain.',triggers:'A proposal could fit multiple spaces · A page is accumulating mixed responsibilities · Similar concepts are being duplicated',workflows:'Place a new concept in the architecture · Resolve an overlap or category conflict',actions:'Classifies the proposal by purpose and lifecycle, writes inclusion and exclusion rules, and records the closest alternatives considered.',evidence:'Working synthesis informed by IA taxonomy and systems-analysis practice · Validate through classification exercises and later retrieval success'},
        {name:'Taxonomy, labeling, and navigation',status:'Synthesized',definition:'Create labels, groupings, and routes that reflect how people expect to find and understand the system’s contents.',triggers:'People search using different language · A label requires explanation · Users open the wrong space or miss related material',workflows:'Frame the system’s concept model · Maintain the shared taxonomy and boundaries · Explain the architecture to contributors',actions:'Collects user language, tests candidate groupings and labels, checks cross-links and navigation, and keeps internal terminology from leaking into user-facing structure.',evidence:'Synthesized from IA practice and GOV.UK card-sorting guidance · Validate with card sorts, tree tests, search logs, and observed navigation'},
        {name:'Dependency mapping and change impact',status:'Synthesized',definition:'Trace how a structural or content change affects related personas, skills, playbooks, docs, decisions, and navigation.',triggers:'A high-reuse concept changes · A new space or relationship is introduced · A revision may make other records stale',workflows:'Coordinate cross-space updates · Reconcile drift after a change',actions:'Maps direct and indirect dependents, distinguishes required from optional updates, and creates a bounded reconciliation queue.',evidence:'Working synthesis informed by systems integration and change-management practice · Validate through impact reviews and reconciliation outcomes'},
        {name:'Decision communication and rationale documentation',status:'Synthesized',definition:'Make architectural choices understandable by recording context, alternatives, tradeoffs, consequences, and revision conditions.',triggers:'A boundary decision will be revisited · Contributors disagree about placement · A temporary exception risks becoming precedent',workflows:'Resolve an overlap or category conflict · Explain the architecture to contributors',actions:'Writes a concise decision record, separates evidence from assumptions, names ownership, and states what future evidence would change the choice.',evidence:'Synthesized from Architectural Decision Records practice · Validate by asking an uninvolved contributor to explain the decision'},
        {name:'Facilitation and cross-functional alignment',status:'Synthesized',definition:'Help people with different mental models reach a shared, evidence-informed understanding of how the system should be shaped.',triggers:'Product, design, engineering, and content use different terms · A new area has competing owners · The model affects several workflows',workflows:'Explain the architecture to contributors · Coordinate cross-space updates',actions:'Surfaces the decision to be made, brings the right examples and evidence, makes disagreement concrete, and closes with owners and next steps.',evidence:'Synthesized from cross-functional design and systems practice · Validate through workshop outcomes and follow-through'},
        {name:'Multi-perspective skill synthesis',status:'Synthesized',definition:'Combine bounded perspectives from multiple personas into one reusable capability without flattening their differences or duplicating existing skills.',triggers:'A capability spans several roles · One perspective leaves a quality gap · A proposed skill could be either a new capability or a composition of existing ones',workflows:'Coordinate perspectives and form the skill · Formalize and integrate the reusable skill',actions:'Selects relevant personas, compares their workflows and quality signals, resolves overlap, and records what is shared, role-specific, and still uncertain.',evidence:'Working synthesis informed by multi-persona orchestration, information architecture, and skill-formation practice · Validate through cross-persona review and later reuse'}
      ]
